1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,349
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 12.

2
00:00:04,969 --> 00:00:10,106
Which code correctly decides whether all five elements of A one and A two are equal?

3
00:00:11,806 --> 00:00:16,200
The correct pattern: assume equal, then look for one counter example.

4
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:18,188
One mismatch is enough to stop.

5
00:00:18,807 --> 00:00:22,521
Now check the others. Starting at false, and setting true on any

6
00:00:22,521 --> 00:00:25,244
match, only proves that one element matches.

7
00:00:25,864 --> 00:00:29,938
Breaking on the first mismatch without setting false leaves eq true.

8
00:00:29,938 --> 00:00:34,936
And A one equals equals A two compares the arrays' addresses, not their contents.

9
00:00:36,536 --> 00:00:42,121
The theory: in C plus plus, an array name decays to a pointer to its first element.

10
00:00:42,121 --> 00:00:45,483
So A one equals equals A two asks whether they are the same

11
00:00:45,483 --> 00:00:49,358
array in memory, which is false for two different arrays.

12
00:00:49,358 --> 00:00:51,808
You must compare element by element.

13
00:00:53,408 --> 00:00:56,018
The correct code sets eq to true, then sets it

14
00:00:56,018 --> 00:00:58,420
false and breaks on the first mismatch.

15
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:02,107
Writing A one equals equals A two for arrays.

16
00:01:02,107 --> 00:01:05,360
It compiles, but it never compares the elements.

17
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:08,973
Exam tip: for all elements questions, start

18
00:01:08,973 --> 00:01:11,696
the flag true and only ever set it false.

19
00:01:11,696 --> 00:01:15,724
For any element questions, start false and only set it true.
